apple-firmware-script for Debian
===============================

This package repackages the upstream t2linux firmware helper
(`docs/tools/firmware.sh` from https://github.com/t2linux/wiki) for Debian.

The pristine upstream source is shipped as documentation at
`/usr/share/doc/apple-firmware-script/upstream-firmware.sh` (also present in
the source tree as `upstream/firmware.sh`). The installed helper
`/usr/libexec/get-apple-firmware` is a minimal, reviewed patch of that
source; the patch is limited to (a) pinning the runtime-downloaded helper
code to an immutable commit and (b) verifying its SHA-256 before execution.
A `/usr/bin/get-apple-firmware` symlink is provided.

Upstream source pin
-------------------
commit `c16ca396f638727f943d577a24dcc0d7e5f0cdd8`, path `docs/tools/firmware.sh`,
sha256 `c1c1d8aa25bb5f089e46ccd0d9738fc13bfbd784f499aa924466c690f059961e`
(31831 bytes).

The script carries an in-file MIT grant (lines 3-24) and a nested Python
`SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT` tag. The embedded Python archive renamer is
derived from acidanthera/OpenCorePkg's `macrecovery` (BSD-3-Clause); both
grants are reproduced in `debian/copyright`. See `SOURCE-MANIFEST.md` in the
installed documentation directory for the full provenance and rights audit,
including the pinned identity of the nested code asset that the optional
`get_from_online` path fetches at run time.

Runtime-downloaded code
-----------------------
The optional `get_from_online` path downloads helper code at run time. The
installed helper pins that fetch to an immutable commit and verifies the
SHA-256 of the downloaded bytes before executing them; a mismatch aborts
without execution. The downloaded code is NOT bundled in this package and
its license is NOT asserted by this package; see `SOURCE-MANIFEST.md`.

Version rationale
-----------------
The upstream source bytes differ from the previously shipped vendor payload
(`apple-firmware-script` 1.4-3). Because this package ships new upstream
bytes, it uses a distinct date-based version (`20261015+ncz1`) rather than
reusing `1.4-3`.

Testing
-------
Offline, non-hardware tests live in `tests/` and run at build time via
`debian/rules`. They never download Apple firmware, mount images, or touch
live devices. They exercise only extracted functions with mocked commands.
